// The Modern World, part 2: work, digital life, health and science today, and global pop culture — the gig economy, remote work, data privacy, COVID-19, CRISPR, mRNA vaccines, anime, internet memes and esports.
ADDART({id:"gigeconomy",t:"The Gig Economy",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🛵",w:"Gig worker",r:["labor","smartphone","ecommerce","remotework","inequality"],
p:[["Work by the task","In the gig economy, people do short jobs, or gigs, often found through apps."],["Riders and freelancers","Delivery riders, ride-hailing drivers and online freelancers are gig workers."],["Flexible but uncertain","Gig work offers flexibility but often lacks job security and benefits."]],
l:"The gig economy is the part of the economy in which people work on short-term tasks or projects, called gigs, rather than in permanent jobs, often finding work through smartphone apps and websites. It includes ride-hailing drivers, food delivery riders, couriers, home cleaners and online freelancers such as writers, designers, programmers and virtual assistants. Gig work gives millions of people flexible ways to earn money, but many gig workers have unstable incomes and lack benefits such as health insurance, paid leave and pensions, leading to debates about their rights.",
s:[["How it works","Digital platforms connect customers with workers. A customer orders a ride, a meal or a design through an app; the platform assigns the job, sets or suggests the price, handles payment and takes a commission. Workers are usually treated as independent contractors rather than employees."],["Ride-hailing and delivery","Uber, founded in 2009, popularized ride-hailing through apps. In Southeast Asia, Grab, founded in 2012, offers rides, food delivery and payments. Food and parcel delivery apps employ huge numbers of riders on motorcycles and bicycles in cities around the world."],["Online freelancing","Websites such as Upwork and Fiverr let businesses hire freelancers anywhere in the world for tasks such as writing, design, translation, coding and customer support. The Philippines has become one of the world’s leading sources of online freelancers and virtual assistants."],["Why people do gig work","Gig work can be flexible, letting people choose their hours, earn extra income, work from home or start working quickly without formal qualifications. For some it is a side job; for others it is their main source of income."]],
k:[["Meaning","Short-term, task-based work, often through apps"],["Examples","Ride-hailing, delivery, online freelancing"],["Uber founded","2009"],["Grab founded","2012"],["Key debate","Are gig workers employees or contractors?"]],
d:[["2008","Online freelancing marketplaces begin to grow rapidly"],["2009","Uber is founded"],["2012","Grab is founded in Malaysia"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic boosts delivery work"],["2021","The UK Supreme Court rules that Uber drivers are workers"],["2024","The European Union adopts rules on platform work"]],
s2:[["Workers’ rights","Because most gig workers are classified as independent contractors, they often do not receive minimum wages, overtime pay, sick leave, health insurance or pensions. Courts and lawmakers in many countries have debated whether some gig workers should be treated as employees. In 2021 the UK Supreme Court ruled that Uber drivers were workers entitled to minimum wage and holiday pay."],["Managed by algorithms","Apps use algorithms to assign tasks, set prices, rate workers and sometimes deactivate accounts. Workers say these systems can be opaque and unfair, with little chance to appeal. New laws in the European Union require more transparency about how platforms manage workers."],["Safety","Delivery riders and drivers face road accidents, long hours, heat, rain and pressure to deliver quickly. Some cities and companies have introduced insurance, rest areas and safety training."],["Gig work in the Philippines","Many Filipinos work as delivery riders, ride-hailing drivers and motorcycle taxi riders, as well as online freelancers serving clients abroad in fields such as customer support, design and bookkeeping. Lawmakers have proposed measures to protect freelancers and gig workers’ pay and benefits."],["Debates","Supporters say the gig economy creates opportunities, flexibility and convenient services. Critics argue that it shifts risks from companies to workers and weakens labor protections. Many people seek a middle path that keeps flexibility while guaranteeing basic protections."],["Where the word comes from","The word gig was long used by musicians for a single performance or engagement. It came to describe short-term jobs more broadly, and the term gig economy became popular around 2009, after the global financial crisis, when many people turned to freelance work."]],
sp:["The virtual assistant capital","With strong English skills, a large young workforce and long experience in call centers, the Philippines has become one of the world’s leading sources of virtual assistants and online freelancers. From homes and coworking spaces in Manila, Cebu, Davao and small towns, Filipinos manage email and schedules, answer customers, edit videos, keep books and run social media for businesses in the United States, Australia, Europe and elsewhere. Surveys of freelance platforms have repeatedly ranked the Philippines among the fastest-growing freelance markets in the world."],
g:[["Gig","A short-term job or task"],["Platform","An app or website that connects customers and workers"],["Independent contractor","A self-employed worker paid per job"],["Algorithm","A set of rules a computer follows"]],
f:["Uber was founded in 2009 and Grab in 2012.","Many Filipinos work as online freelancers.","The EU adopted rules on platform work in 2024."],
fun:"The word gig originally meant a musician’s single performance.",
tl:"Ang gig economy ay ang paggawa ng maiikling trabaho o gig sa pamamagitan ng mga app, gaya ng pagde-deliver, pagmamaneho at online freelancing. Flexible ito ngunit kadalasan ay walang benepisyo at seguridad sa trabaho."});

ADDART({id:"remotework",t:"Remote Work",c:"Economics and Society",i:"💻",w:"Remote work",r:["internet","cloud","gigeconomy","covid19","urban"],
p:[["Working from anywhere","Remote work means doing a job away from the office, often from home."],["A pandemic shift","The COVID-19 pandemic made remote work common almost overnight."],["Home and office","Many workers now split their time between home and office."]],
l:"Remote work, also called telework or working from home, is doing a job from a place other than a central workplace, usually using computers, the internet and video calls. Long possible for some jobs, it became widespread almost overnight in 2020, when the COVID-19 pandemic closed offices around the world. Today many people work fully remotely or in hybrid arrangements, splitting their week between home and office. Remote work can save commuting time and widen job opportunities, but it also raises questions about productivity, isolation, fairness and the future of cities.",
s:[["Beginnings","In 1973 the American engineer Jack Nilles coined the word telecommuting, imagining that telecommunications could replace the daily commute. Over the following decades, personal computers, the internet, email and mobile phones made working from home increasingly practical for some jobs."],["The pandemic shift","When the COVID-19 pandemic struck in 2020, governments ordered lockdowns and companies sent office workers home. Video calling apps saw enormous growth: daily participants in Zoom meetings rose from about 10 million in December 2019 to about 300 million by April 2020."],["Hybrid work","After the pandemic, many companies adopted hybrid work, with employees working some days at home and others in the office. Others called workers back to offices full-time, while some stayed fully remote. The best arrangement is widely debated."],["Tools","Remote workers rely on laptops, fast internet, video calls, chat apps, shared online documents and cloud storage. Project management apps help teams coordinate across locations and time zones."]],
k:[["Also called","Telework, working from home"],["Word telecommuting coined","1973"],["Big shift","2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic"],["Philippine law","Telecommuting Act (2018)"],["Common today","Hybrid work"]],
d:[["1973","Jack Nilles coins the word telecommuting"],["1990s","Email and the internet make remote work easier"],["2018","The Philippines passes the Telecommuting Act"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic makes remote work widespread"],["2020","Estonia launches one of the first digital nomad visas"],["2025","The Philippines creates a digital nomad visa"]],
s2:[["Benefits","Remote work can save hours of commuting, reduce traffic and pollution, give workers more flexibility, help parents and caregivers and allow people with disabilities to work more easily. Companies can hire talent from anywhere and save on office space."],["Challenges","Remote workers may feel isolated, find it hard to separate work from home life or struggle with poor internet or crowded homes. Managers worry about communication, teamwork, training new staff and building company culture. Studies of productivity have found mixed results, depending on the job and the arrangement."],["Not for everyone","Many jobs, such as those of nurses, factory workers, farmers, drivers, cooks and shop staff, cannot be done remotely. The rise of remote work has highlighted differences between people who can work from home and those who cannot."],["Remote work in the Philippines","The Philippines passed the Telecommuting Act in 2018, giving employees who work from home the same rights as office workers. During the pandemic, many companies, including business process outsourcing firms, shifted to work-from-home arrangements. Heavy traffic in Metro Manila makes remote work especially attractive to many workers."],["Digital nomads","Digital nomads are remote workers who travel while working online, often living in different countries for months at a time. Many countries, including Estonia, Thailand, Indonesia and the Philippines, which created a digital nomad visa in 2025, now offer special visas to attract them."],["Cities after the pandemic","With fewer people commuting to offices every day, some city centers have seen empty office buildings and quieter streets, while suburbs and smaller towns have grown. Cities are rethinking how to use office space, sometimes converting it into homes."]],
sp:["The man who named telecommuting","In 1973, during a global oil crisis that made fuel scarce and expensive, Jack Nilles, a former NASA rocket engineer working at the University of Southern California, began studying whether people could work at home or in nearby offices using telecommunications instead of driving to work. He coined the words telecommuting and telework and led an experiment with an insurance company in which employees worked from satellite offices closer to home. Nilles predicted that computers would one day make remote work common, nearly half a century before the COVID-19 pandemic proved him right."],
g:[["Remote work","Working away from a central workplace"],["Hybrid work","Splitting work time between home and office"],["Telecommuting","Working from home using telecommunications"],["Digital nomad","A remote worker who travels while working"]],
f:["The word telecommuting was coined in 1973.","Remote work became widespread in 2020.","The Philippines passed a Telecommuting Act in 2018."],
fun:"Daily Zoom meeting participants grew about thirtyfold in four months in 2020.",
tl:"Ang remote work ay paggawa ng trabaho nang malayo sa opisina, kadalasan sa bahay. Naging karaniwan ito noong pandemya ng COVID-19 noong 2020. Marami ngayon ang hybrid, na nagtatrabaho sa bahay at sa opisina."});

ADDART({id:"dataprivacy",t:"Data Privacy",c:"Technology",i:"🔐",w:"Information privacy",r:["cybersecurity","internet","socialmedia","smartphone","hrights"],
p:[["Your personal data","Data privacy is about who can collect, use and share information about you."],["Everything leaves a trace","Apps, websites and devices collect large amounts of personal data."],["Laws and habits","Privacy laws and good online habits help protect personal information."]],
l:"Data privacy, also called information privacy, is the right of people to control how information about them is collected, used, stored and shared. Every day, websites, apps, phones, cameras and smart devices collect personal data such as names, locations, contacts, purchases, photos, health information and online behavior. This data powers useful services and advertising, but it can also be misused, sold, leaked or used for surveillance. Laws such as the European Union’s General Data Protection Regulation and the Philippines’ Data Privacy Act of 2012 set rules for how organizations handle personal data.",
s:[["What is personal data?","Personal data is any information that can identify a person, directly or indirectly, such as a name, address, phone number, ID number, photo, location, fingerprints or online account. Sensitive data includes health records, religion, political views, finances and information about children."],["Who collects data","Social media companies, search engines, online shops, banks, phone companies, governments, schools and employers all collect data. Many apps track location and activity. Data brokers buy and sell information about people, often without their knowledge."],["Why it matters","Leaked or misused data can lead to identity theft, scams, discrimination, embarrassment, stalking or political manipulation. Privacy is also linked to freedom: people may not speak or act freely if they feel constantly watched."],["Privacy laws","The EU’s General Data Protection Regulation, in force since 2018, gives people rights to see, correct and delete their data and allows large fines for violations. The Philippines’ Data Privacy Act of 2012 created the National Privacy Commission, which enforces rules on how personal data is processed."]],
k:[["Philippine law","Data Privacy Act of 2012"],["Philippine regulator","National Privacy Commission"],["EU law","General Data Protection Regulation (2018)"],["Data Privacy Day","28 January"],["Key habit","Think before sharing personal information"]],
d:[["1981","The Council of Europe adopts the first international data protection treaty"],["2012","The Philippines passes the Data Privacy Act"],["2013","Edward Snowden reveals mass surveillance programs"],["2014","A European court recognizes a right to be forgotten"],["2018","The Cambridge Analytica scandal breaks"],["2018","The EU’s General Data Protection Regulation takes effect"]],
s2:[["Cambridge Analytica","In 2018 it was revealed that the political consulting firm Cambridge Analytica had obtained data from up to 87 million Facebook users, many of whom never agreed to share it, through a personality quiz app. The Philippines had one of the largest numbers of affected users. The scandal led to investigations, fines and greater public concern about data privacy."],["Data breaches","Data breaches happen when hackers steal data or organizations accidentally expose it. In 2016, a breach of the Philippine Commission on Elections’ website exposed personal information of tens of millions of registered voters, one of the largest government data breaches in the world at the time."],["Surveillance","Governments collect data to fight crime and terrorism, but mass surveillance raises concerns about civil liberties. In 2013 the former US intelligence contractor Edward Snowden revealed secret programs collecting phone and internet data worldwide, sparking global debate."],["The right to be forgotten","In 2014 the Court of Justice of the European Union ruled that people can ask search engines to remove links to outdated or irrelevant personal information, known as the right to be forgotten. Supporters see it as protecting privacy; critics worry about limiting access to information."],["Protecting your privacy","Tips include using strong, unique passwords and two-factor authentication; checking app permissions; thinking before posting personal details, locations or photos; being cautious with quizzes and free Wi-Fi; reading privacy settings; and never sharing one-time codes. Children should ask a trusted adult before sharing personal information online."],["Privacy and artificial intelligence","AI systems are trained on huge amounts of data, sometimes including personal information and images collected from the internet. Face recognition and AI that can imitate voices raise new privacy concerns, and regulators are working on rules for AI and data."]],
sp:["If it’s free, you may be the product","Many online services, such as social media, search engines and free apps, cost users nothing. Instead, they earn money mainly by showing advertisements, which are more valuable when targeted to each user’s interests. To do this, they collect detailed information about what people search, watch, like, buy and where they go. A popular saying sums it up: if you are not paying for the product, you are the product. While this is a simplification, it reminds users that their attention and data have value, and that understanding how free services make money helps people make informed choices."],
g:[["Personal data","Information that can identify a person"],["Data breach","An incident in which data is stolen or exposed"],["Surveillance","Close watching of people, often by governments or companies"],["Two-factor authentication","A login that requires two forms of proof"]],
f:["The Philippines passed the Data Privacy Act in 2012.","The EU’s GDPR took effect in 2018.","Data Privacy Day is 28 January."],
fun:"The first international treaty on data protection was signed in 1981, long before smartphones.",
tl:"Ang data privacy ay ang karapatang kontrolin kung paano kinokolekta at ginagamit ang impormasyon tungkol sa iyo. Sa Pilipinas, pinoprotektahan ito ng Data Privacy Act of 2012. Gumamit ng matibay na password at mag-isip bago magbahagi online."});

ADDART({id:"covid19",t:"The COVID-19 Pandemic",c:"Health and the Body",i:"😷",w:"COVID-19 pandemic",r:["pandemic","vacc","mrna","immune","remotework"],
p:[["A global pandemic","COVID-19 spread around the world from 2020, changing daily life everywhere."],["Millions of deaths","Millions of people died, and many more fell ill."],["Vaccines in record time","Vaccines were developed and given to billions of people within about two years."]],
l:"The COVID-19 pandemic was a worldwide outbreak of coronavirus disease 2019, caused by the virus SARS-CoV-2, first identified in Wuhan, China, in December 2019. It spread to almost every country, and the World Health Organization declared it a pandemic on 11 March 2020. More than 7 million deaths were officially reported, and the WHO estimated that the true number of excess deaths in 2020 and 2021 alone was around 15 million. Lockdowns, school closures and travel bans transformed daily life, while vaccines developed in record time saved millions of lives. The WHO ended the global emergency in May 2023.",
s:[["The virus","SARS-CoV-2 is a coronavirus, a family of viruses named for the crown-like spikes on their surface. It spreads mainly through the air when infected people breathe, talk, cough or sneeze, especially indoors. Symptoms range from none or mild, like a cold, to severe pneumonia and death, with older people and those with certain health conditions at highest risk."],["Spread and response","After the first cases in Wuhan, the virus spread rapidly through travel. Countries responded with lockdowns, closures of schools and businesses, travel restrictions, testing, contact tracing, quarantine, mask wearing and physical distancing. Hospitals in many places were overwhelmed."],["Vaccines","Scientists developed vaccines in under a year, building on decades of research. The first vaccinations outside trials began in the United Kingdom in December 2020. Vaccines using mRNA, viral vectors and inactivated virus were given to billions of people, greatly reducing severe illness and deaths, though access was unequal between rich and poor countries."],["Variants","As the virus spread, it mutated into new variants, such as Delta in 2021 and Omicron, identified in November 2021, which spread more easily. Updated vaccines and earlier infections gave many people some immunity, and the disease became less deadly for most."]],
k:[["Cause","The SARS-CoV-2 virus"],["First identified","Wuhan, China, December 2019"],["Declared a pandemic","11 March 2020"],["Reported deaths","More than 7 million"],["Global emergency ended","5 May 2023"]],
d:[["2019","The first cases are identified in Wuhan, China"],["2020","The WHO declares a pandemic on 11 March"],["2020","The Philippines places Luzon under enhanced community quarantine"],["2020","The first COVID-19 vaccinations begin in the United Kingdom"],["2021","The Omicron variant is identified"],["2023","The WHO ends the COVID-19 global health emergency"]],
s2:[["The pandemic in the Philippines","The Philippines confirmed its first case in January 2020. In March 2020 the government placed Luzon under an enhanced community quarantine, one of the strictest and longest lockdowns in the world, followed by different levels of community quarantine for many months. Schools moved to distance learning for about two years. Vaccinations began in March 2021. Frontline health workers were hailed as heroes."],["Effects on daily life","Billions of people stayed home. Offices shifted to remote work, students to online and modular learning, and shopping, worship and celebrations moved online. Many people lost jobs and income, and the pandemic increased poverty, hunger, mental health problems and learning losses, especially for poorer families."],["Long COVID","Some people continue to have symptoms such as tiredness, breathlessness and difficulty concentrating for months after infection, a condition known as long COVID. Researchers are still studying its causes and treatments."],["Where did it come from?","The origin of SARS-CoV-2 is still debated. Many scientists think the virus most likely spread to humans from animals, possibly at a market in Wuhan, as earlier coronaviruses did. Others consider it possible that it escaped from a laboratory in Wuhan that studied coronaviruses. Investigations have not reached a definitive conclusion."],["Misinformation","False claims about the virus, cures and vaccines spread widely online, which the WHO called an infodemic. Health authorities worked to share accurate information, while debates over lockdowns, masks and vaccine mandates became politically divisive in many countries."],["Lessons for the future","The pandemic exposed weaknesses in health systems and global cooperation. In 2025 member states of the World Health Organization adopted a Pandemic Agreement aiming to improve preparedness and fairer access to vaccines and medicines in future pandemics."]],
sp:["The first vaccine shot","On 8 December 2020, at a hospital in Coventry, England, a 90-year-old grandmother named Margaret Keenan became the first person in the world to receive an approved COVID-19 vaccine outside a clinical trial. Wearing a blue Christmas T-shirt with a penguin, she received the Pfizer-BioNTech vaccine and said it was the best early birthday present she could wish for. The second person vaccinated that morning was a man named William Shakespeare, a coincidence that delighted the media. Within two years, billions of vaccine doses had been given around the world."],
g:[["Pandemic","An outbreak of disease that spreads across many countries"],["Coronavirus","A family of viruses with crown-like spikes"],["Variant","A version of a virus with changes in its genes"],["Quarantine","Keeping people apart to stop disease spreading"]],
f:["The WHO declared COVID-19 a pandemic on 11 March 2020.","More than 7 million deaths were officially reported.","The global emergency ended in May 2023."],
fun:"The second person to receive an approved COVID-19 vaccine was named William Shakespeare.",
tl:"Ang pandemya ng COVID-19 ay pandaigdigang pagkalat ng sakit na dulot ng virus na SARS-CoV-2 mula 2020. Milyun-milyon ang namatay. Nagkaroon ng mga lockdown, at nakagawa ng bakuna sa loob ng isang taon."});

ADDART({id:"crispr",t:"Gene Editing and CRISPR",c:"Science",i:"✂️",w:"CRISPR gene editing",r:["dna","genome","mendel","microbe","bloodtypes"],
p:[["Editing life’s code","CRISPR is a tool that lets scientists change DNA precisely."],["Borrowed from bacteria","It comes from a natural defense system bacteria use against viruses."],["Hope and caution","Gene editing offers new cures but raises serious ethical questions."]],
l:"Gene editing is the technology of making precise changes to the DNA of living things. The most famous tool, CRISPR-Cas9, adapted from a natural defense system in bacteria, acts like molecular scissors that can cut DNA at a chosen place, allowing genes to be disabled, repaired or changed. Developed in 2012 by Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier, who won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 2020, CRISPR has transformed biology and medicine. The first CRISPR-based treatment, for sickle cell disease, was approved in 2023. Gene editing also raises serious ethical questions, especially about editing human embryos.",
s:[["Bacteria’s defense","Scientists noticed strange repeated patterns in bacterial DNA as early as 1987 and later named them CRISPR. They discovered that bacteria store pieces of viral DNA in these patterns as a memory of past infections and use enzymes such as Cas9 to recognize and cut the DNA of returning viruses."],["Turning it into a tool","In 2012 Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier showed that CRISPR-Cas9 could be programmed with a guide molecule to cut any chosen DNA sequence. Soon after, scientists including Feng Zhang showed it worked in human and animal cells. The tool was cheaper, faster and easier than earlier gene-editing methods."],["How it works","A short guide RNA, designed to match a target DNA sequence, leads the Cas9 enzyme to the right spot, where it cuts both strands of DNA. When the cell repairs the cut, scientists can disable a gene or insert a new sequence. Newer methods, called base editing and prime editing, can change single DNA letters without cutting both strands."],["Uses in research","CRISPR lets scientists switch genes on and off to learn what they do, create models of diseases and study how cells work, speeding up research across biology."]],
k:[["Main tool","CRISPR-Cas9"],["Origin","A bacterial defense system"],["Developed as a tool","2012"],["Nobel Prize","Doudna and Charpentier (Chemistry, 2020)"],["First approved CRISPR therapy","2023, for sickle cell disease"]],
d:[["1987","Scientists notice repeated DNA patterns in bacteria"],["2002","The name CRISPR is coined"],["2012","Doudna and Charpentier show CRISPR can edit DNA"],["2018","A scientist announces the birth of gene-edited babies"],["2020","Doudna and Charpentier win the Nobel Prize in Chemistry"],["2023","The first CRISPR-based medicine is approved"]],
s2:[["Treating disease","In 2023 the United Kingdom and then the United States approved Casgevy, the first medicine using CRISPR, to treat sickle cell disease and beta thalassemia, inherited blood disorders. Doctors edit patients’ own blood stem cells and return them to the body. In 2025, doctors in the United States treated a baby with a rare inherited disease using a gene-editing therapy designed just for him."],["Crops and animals","Gene editing can produce crops that resist disease, tolerate drought or have better nutrition, sometimes faster than traditional breeding. Countries regulate gene-edited crops differently; the Philippines adopted rules in 2022 for evaluating gene-edited products, and scientists there are working on improved rice and other crops."],["The gene-edited babies","In November 2018, the Chinese scientist He Jiankui announced that twin girls had been born after he edited their genes as embryos to try to make them resistant to HIV. Scientists around the world condemned the experiment as unsafe and unethical, and he was sentenced to prison in China."],["Ethics","Editing genes in a patient’s body cells affects only that person, but editing embryos, eggs or sperm creates changes that could be passed to future generations. Many scientists and countries oppose such heritable editing, at least for now, because of safety risks and fears of designer babies and new inequalities."],["Fair access","Gene-editing treatments are currently extremely expensive, raising concerns that only the wealthy will benefit. Sickle cell disease is most common among people of African, Middle Eastern and South Asian descent, many in low-income countries, making affordable access an important issue."],["Gene drives","Scientists are exploring gene drives, which spread an edited gene through a whole population, for example to reduce mosquitoes that carry malaria. Because such changes could spread through wild populations, they are being studied very carefully."]],
sp:["The yogurt connection","Some of the key early discoveries about CRISPR came from an unexpected place: the yogurt and cheese industry. Dairy companies use bacteria to ferment milk, and viruses that attack these bacteria can ruin whole batches. In 2007, scientists working for the food company Danisco showed that bacteria used to make yogurt gained immunity to viruses by adding pieces of viral DNA to their CRISPR regions. Their work proved that CRISPR is a bacterial immune system and helped dairy companies breed hardier bacteria, paving the way for the gene-editing revolution."],
g:[["Gene editing","Making precise changes to DNA"],["CRISPR","A bacterial defense system used as a gene-editing tool"],["Cas9","An enzyme that cuts DNA"],["Heritable","Able to be passed on to offspring"]],
f:["CRISPR comes from a bacterial defense system.","Doudna and Charpentier won the 2020 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.","The first CRISPR medicine was approved in 2023."],
fun:"Early CRISPR discoveries came from scientists studying yogurt bacteria.",
tl:"Ang CRISPR ay kasangkapan na nagpapahintulot sa mga siyentipiko na baguhin nang eksakto ang DNA. Nagmula ito sa likas na depensa ng bakterya laban sa virus. Nakatutulong ito sa paggamot ng sakit ngunit may mga tanong sa etika."});

ADDART({id:"mrna",t:"mRNA Vaccines",c:"Health and the Body",i:"💉",w:"mRNA vaccine",r:["vacc","covid19","immune","dna","crispr"],
p:[["A new kind of vaccine","mRNA vaccines teach the body to make a harmless piece of a germ."],["Fast to make","They can be designed and produced much faster than many older vaccines."],["Decades of work","They were built on decades of research by scientists such as Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman."]],
l:"An mRNA vaccine is a type of vaccine that uses messenger RNA, a molecule that carries genetic instructions, to teach cells to make a harmless piece of a virus, usually a protein from its surface. The immune system learns to recognize this protein and is prepared if the real virus attacks. The first mRNA vaccines, against COVID-19, were authorized in December 2020, less than a year after the virus was identified, and were given to hundreds of millions of people. They were made possible by decades of research, including discoveries by Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman, who won the 2023 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.",
s:[["What mRNA does","In every cell, DNA stores genetic instructions, and messenger RNA carries copies of these instructions to the cell’s protein-making machinery. mRNA is temporary and breaks down naturally after a short time; it does not enter the cell nucleus or change a person’s DNA."],["How the vaccines work","An mRNA vaccine contains instructions for a viral protein, such as the spike protein of the COVID-19 virus, wrapped in tiny fat bubbles called lipid nanoparticles. After injection, cells read the instructions and make the protein, which the immune system recognizes as foreign, producing antibodies and immune cells."],["A long road","Scientists experimented with mRNA from the 1990s, but it caused harmful inflammation and broke down quickly. In 2005 Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman showed that changing one of the building blocks of mRNA prevented the inflammatory reaction. Improvements in lipid nanoparticles helped deliver mRNA into cells."],["COVID-19 vaccines","When the genetic code of the COVID-19 virus was published in January 2020, companies such as BioNTech, working with Pfizer, and Moderna designed vaccines within days. After large trials, they were authorized in December 2020 and proved highly effective at preventing severe illness."]],
k:[["Uses","Messenger RNA instructions"],["First authorized","December 2020, for COVID-19"],["Key discovery","Karikó and Weissman (2005)"],["Nobel Prize","Physiology or Medicine, 2023"],["Delivery","Lipid nanoparticles"]],
d:[["1961","Messenger RNA is discovered"],["1990","Scientists show mRNA injected into mice can make proteins"],["2005","Karikó and Weissman publish their key discovery"],["2020","The first mRNA vaccines are authorized for COVID-19"],["2023","Karikó and Weissman win the Nobel Prize"],["2024","An mRNA vaccine for another disease, RSV, is approved"]],
s2:[["Katalin Karikó","The Hungarian-born biochemist Katalin Karikó spent decades working on mRNA, often with little funding and recognition. She was demoted at her university and repeatedly turned down for grants. Her persistence, and her partnership with the immunologist Drew Weissman, made mRNA vaccines possible, and in 2023 they shared the Nobel Prize."],["Speed and flexibility","Because mRNA vaccines are made from a genetic code rather than grown in eggs or cells, they can be designed quickly and updated when viruses change. COVID-19 vaccines were updated for new variants, and scientists hope the platform can respond quickly to future pandemics."],["Safety","mRNA vaccines went through large clinical trials and continued monitoring of billions of doses. Most side effects, such as a sore arm, tiredness or fever, are mild and short-lived. A rare side effect, inflammation of the heart muscle called myocarditis, was found mainly in young men, usually mild. Health agencies concluded that for most people the benefits outweighed the risks."],["Beyond COVID-19","Researchers are developing mRNA vaccines and treatments for influenza, RSV, which was approved in 2024, HIV, malaria, tuberculosis and cancer. Personalized cancer vaccines, designed for each patient’s tumor, are being tested in trials."],["Challenges","Early mRNA vaccines needed very cold storage, which made distribution difficult in hot countries with limited electricity. Newer versions are more stable. Unequal access to vaccines during the pandemic also led to efforts to build vaccine factories in Africa, Asia and Latin America."],["Hesitancy","Some people were hesitant to take mRNA vaccines because the technology was new, and misinformation spread online, including false claims that the vaccines change DNA. Health workers and scientists worked to explain how the vaccines work."]],
sp:["Designed in a weekend","On 10 January 2020, Chinese scientists published the genetic sequence of the new coronavirus online. At BioNTech in Germany, the husband-and-wife founders Uğur Şahin and Özlem Türeci, both children of Turkish immigrants, read the reports and quickly redirected their company, which had been working on cancer vaccines, to the new virus, designing vaccine candidates within days. At Moderna in the United States, scientists finalized the design of their vaccine just two days after the sequence was published. Less than a year later, both vaccines were being given to people around the world."],
g:[["mRNA","Messenger RNA, a molecule that carries genetic instructions"],["Spike protein","A protein on the surface of the COVID-19 virus"],["Lipid nanoparticle","A tiny fat bubble that carries mRNA into cells"],["Antibody","A protein that helps the body fight infection"]],
f:["The first mRNA vaccines were authorized in December 2020.","mRNA does not change a person’s DNA.","Karikó and Weissman won the 2023 Nobel Prize."],
fun:"Moderna’s COVID-19 vaccine was designed two days after the virus’s genetic code was published.",
tl:"Ang mRNA vaccine ay uri ng bakuna na nagtuturo sa mga selula na gumawa ng hindi mapanganib na bahagi ng virus upang matuto ang immune system. Ginamit ito laban sa COVID-19 mula Disyembre 2020."});

ADDART({id:"anime",t:"Anime and Manga",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎌",w:"Anime",r:["anim","comics","japancountry","hokusai","streaming","manga"],
p:[["Japanese animation","Anime is the distinctive animation style that comes from Japan."],["Comics first","Many anime are based on manga, Japanese comics."],["Loved worldwide","Anime has fans in almost every country, including the Philippines."]],
l:"Anime is animation from Japan, known for its distinctive art styles, expressive characters and wide range of stories, from adventure and science fiction to romance, sports, comedy and drama, made for children and adults alike. Many anime are adapted from manga, Japanese comics, which are read by people of all ages. Beginning with television series such as Astro Boy in 1963, anime grew into a global phenomenon, with films by Studio Ghibli, series such as Pokémon, Dragon Ball, One Piece and Demon Slayer and fans around the world. In the Philippines, anime shows such as Voltes V became part of popular culture.",
s:[["Manga","Manga are Japanese comics, usually printed in black and white and read from right to left. They cover every genre and are read by children and adults. Japan’s manga tradition draws on centuries of illustrated stories, and the artist Hokusai used the word manga for his sketchbooks in the 1800s."],["The god of manga","Osamu Tezuka, often called the god of manga, created Astro Boy, about a robot boy, first as a manga in the 1950s and then as Japan’s first popular weekly animated television series in 1963. His big-eyed characters and cinematic storytelling shaped the style of anime and manga."],["A global boom","From the 1970s and 1980s, anime spread abroad on television. Series such as Voltes V, Dragon Ball, Sailor Moon and Pokémon gained huge followings, and the film Akira, released in 1988, impressed audiences worldwide. Today streaming services make anime available almost everywhere."],["Studio Ghibli","Studio Ghibli, founded in 1985 by Hayao Miyazaki, Isao Takahata and Toshio Suzuki, is famous for beautifully hand-drawn films such as My Neighbor Totoro and Princess Mononoke. Spirited Away, released in 2001, won the Academy Award for Best Animated Feature in 2003, and The Boy and the Heron won the same award in 2024."]],
k:[["Origin","Japan"],["Based on","Often manga, Japanese comics"],["Early TV classic","Astro Boy (1963)"],["Famous studio","Studio Ghibli (founded 1985)"],["Oscar winners","Spirited Away, The Boy and the Heron"]],
d:[["1963","Astro Boy becomes Japan’s first popular weekly anime series"],["1977","Voltes V first airs in Japan"],["1985","Studio Ghibli is founded"],["1997","The One Piece manga and Pokémon anime begin"],["2003","Spirited Away wins an Academy Award"],["2020","Demon Slayer: Mugen Train becomes Japan’s highest-grossing film"]],
s2:[["Anime in the Philippines","Anime has been hugely popular in the Philippines since the late 1970s, when robot series such as Voltes V, Mazinger Z and Daimos captured young viewers. Later shows such as Dragon Ball, Ghost Fighter, Slam Dunk and Naruto, dubbed in Filipino, became favorites. In 2023 a Filipino live-action version, Voltes V: Legacy, was produced."],["The Voltes V story","When the Marcos government removed Voltes V and other robot anime from television in 1979, saying they were too violent, many fans were upset. Some later saw the show’s story of rebellion against a tyrannical empire as having political meaning. Voltes V returned to Philippine television after 1986, with a popular rerun in 1999, and its theme song remains famous."],["One Piece","One Piece, a manga by Eiichiro Oda about a young pirate named Luffy searching for treasure, began in 1997 and has sold more copies than any other comic series by a single author, according to Guinness World Records. Its anime has run for more than a thousand episodes."],["Fans and cosplay","Anime fans gather at conventions around the world, where many practice cosplay, dressing up as their favorite characters. Fan communities create art, music and videos and discuss their favorite series online. Enthusiastic fans are sometimes called otaku."],["Industry","The anime industry has grown to be worth trillions of yen, earning a growing share of its income from overseas. However, many animators in Japan work long hours for low pay, and the industry is debating how to improve working conditions."],["Influence","Anime and manga have influenced filmmakers, artists, fashion and video games worldwide. Their stories often explore friendship, perseverance, nature, technology and the struggle between good and evil, themes that resonate across cultures."]],
sp:["Astro Boy’s weekly miracle","When Osamu Tezuka decided to turn his manga Astro Boy into a weekly television series in 1963, experts said it was impossible: full animation took far too long and cost too much. Tezuka found shortcuts. His team reused drawings, moved only characters’ mouths during dialogue, held still images while the camera panned and used fewer drawings per second. These techniques, called limited animation, made weekly anime possible and became part of the style that defines anime today. Astro Boy was a hit, and it was soon broadcast in the United States and other countries."],
g:[["Anime","Animation from Japan"],["Manga","Japanese comics"],["Cosplay","Dressing up as a character from a story"],["Limited animation","Animation using fewer drawings to save time"]],
f:["Astro Boy began as a weekly anime in 1963.","Spirited Away won an Academy Award in 2003.","Voltes V is a beloved anime in the Philippines."],
fun:"Hokusai used the word manga for his sketchbooks almost 200 years ago.",
tl:"Ang anime ay animasyon mula sa Japan, at marami rito ay batay sa manga o komiks ng Hapon. Sikat ito sa buong mundo. Sa Pilipinas, minahal ng maraming henerasyon ang Voltes V, Dragon Ball at iba pa."});

ADDART({id:"memes",t:"Internet Memes",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"😂",w:"Internet meme",r:["socialmedia","internet","misinformation","comics","streaming"],
p:[["Jokes that spread","An internet meme is an image, video or phrase that spreads and changes online."],["Shared culture","Memes let people share jokes and ideas quickly across the world."],["More than jokes","Memes can raise money, spread news and also spread misinformation."]],
l:"An internet meme is an idea, image, video, phrase or style that spreads rapidly online, usually copied and changed by many people along the way. Memes often combine pictures with short captions, but they also include dances, challenges, catchphrases and viral videos. They are a form of shared humor and culture, letting people comment on everyday life, news and politics in a few seconds. The word meme was coined by the biologist Richard Dawkins in 1976. Memes can raise awareness and money for good causes, but they can also spread misinformation and mockery.",
s:[["Where the word comes from","In his 1976 book The Selfish Gene, the British biologist Richard Dawkins coined the word meme for a unit of culture, such as a tune, a catchphrase or a fashion, that spreads from person to person by imitation, the way genes spread by reproduction. The word later came to mean viral internet content."],["How memes work","A meme usually starts with an image, video or phrase. Others copy it, change the words or pictures and share it, creating countless variations. Popular formats are recognized instantly, so a new caption on a familiar image can make people laugh."],["Early internet memes","Among the first internet memes was the Dancing Baby, a 3D animation that spread by email in 1996. In the 2000s, image macros with bold white captions, cat pictures and prank links such as Rickrolling, which tricks people into watching a 1987 music video, became popular."],["Memes everywhere","Today memes spread on social media and messaging apps in every language, including Filipino. They are used in advertising, politics, education and everyday conversation, and businesses and governments sometimes use them to reach young people."]],
k:[["Word coined","1976, by Richard Dawkins"],["Early meme","The Dancing Baby (1996)"],["Famous challenge","Ice Bucket Challenge (2014)"],["Typical form","An image with a caption"],["Spreads by","Copying and changing"]],
d:[["1976","Richard Dawkins coins the word meme"],["1996","The Dancing Baby spreads online"],["2007","Rickrolling becomes a popular prank"],["2013","The Doge meme inspires a cryptocurrency"],["2014","The Ice Bucket Challenge raises money for ALS research"],["2017","The Distracted Boyfriend meme spreads worldwide"]],
s2:[["The Ice Bucket Challenge","In 2014 the Ice Bucket Challenge asked people to film themselves pouring a bucket of ice water over their heads and nominate friends to do the same, or donate to research on ALS, a nerve disease. Millions took part, including celebrities, and the ALS Association received over 100 million US dollars in a few weeks, which helped fund research that identified a new gene linked to the disease."],["Doge","A 2010 photo of Kabosu, a Shiba Inu dog in Japan, glancing sideways became the Doge meme, with captions in broken English such as such wow. It inspired a joke cryptocurrency, Dogecoin, in 2013, which later became worth billions of dollars. Kabosu died in 2024, mourned by fans around the world."],["Memes and misinformation","Because memes are short, funny and easily shared, they can spread false or misleading claims quickly, without sources. They are sometimes used in political campaigns and propaganda. Checking claims before sharing applies to memes too."],["Copyright and consent","Memes often use photos, film clips or images of real people without permission. Some people who became memes found fame and even income, while others suffered embarrassment or harassment. Questions about copyright and respect for people in memes are increasingly discussed."],["Filipino meme culture","Filipino internet users are known for their creativity and humor online, turning celebrities, teleserye scenes, politicians, everyday situations and wordplay in Filipino and English into memes. Memes are part of how many Filipinos react to news, from typhoon class suspensions to sports victories."],["Why memes matter","Researchers study memes as a form of folk culture, like jokes and sayings passed around in earlier times. They show what people care about, laugh at and worry about, and how ideas spread in the digital age."]],
sp:["The dancing baby","In 1996, software developers created a short 3D animation of a baby dancing the cha-cha to show off what their program could do. People began emailing the file to each other, adding music and new versions, and soon the Dancing Baby was everywhere, even appearing on the television series Ally McBeal. In an age before social media, when most people still used dial-up internet, it became one of the first viral internet memes, spreading worldwide simply because people found it funny and shared it."],
g:[["Meme","An idea or piece of content that spreads by imitation"],["Viral","Spreading rapidly from person to person online"],["Format","A recognizable template used for many memes"],["Challenge","An online trend inviting people to do and share an activity"]],
f:["Richard Dawkins coined the word meme in 1976.","The Ice Bucket Challenge raised over $100 million for ALS research.","The Dancing Baby was one of the first internet memes."],
fun:"A dog meme inspired a cryptocurrency that became worth billions.",
tl:"Ang internet meme ay larawan, video o pahayag na mabilis kumalat at binabago ng maraming tao online. Ginagamit ito sa biruan at pagbabahagi ng ideya, ngunit maaari rin itong magkalat ng maling impormasyon."});

ADDART({id:"esports",t:"Esports",c:"Sports",i:"🎮",w:"Esports",r:["videogames","olymp","streaming","phsport","chess"],
p:[["Competitive gaming","Esports are organized video game competitions."],["Huge audiences","Top tournaments fill arenas and are watched online by millions."],["Filipino champions","Philippine teams have won world titles in Mobile Legends."]],
l:"Esports, short for electronic sports, are organized competitions in which individual players or teams compete in video games, often for prizes and in front of large audiences, both in arenas and online. Popular esports games include Mobile Legends: Bang Bang, League of Legends, Dota 2, Counter-Strike, Valorant and fighting games. Professional players train for hours a day, belong to teams with coaches and sponsors and compete in leagues and world championships with millions of dollars in prize money. Esports have become medal events at the Southeast Asian Games and the Asian Games, and the Philippines has become a powerhouse in mobile esports.",
s:[["Early competitions","One of the first video game competitions was held at Stanford University in 1972, when students competed at the game Spacewar! for a year’s subscription to Rolling Stone magazine. In 1980 a Space Invaders championship in the United States attracted about 10,000 players."],["The rise of esports","In the late 1990s and 2000s, online games such as StarCraft became hugely popular in South Korea, where professional players became celebrities and matches were shown on television. The growth of internet streaming in the 2010s allowed millions to watch tournaments live online."],["Popular games","Esports include many types of games: multiplayer online battle arena games such as League of Legends, Dota 2 and Mobile Legends, where teams of five battle to destroy each other’s bases; shooting games such as Counter-Strike and Valorant; fighting games; sports games; and strategy games."],["Life as a pro","Professional esports players train long hours, studying strategies and practicing teamwork, often living together in gaming houses. Careers can be short, as reflexes peak young. Teams employ coaches, analysts, nutritionists and psychologists."]],
k:[["Meaning","Organized video game competitions"],["Early tournament","Spacewar!, Stanford, 1972"],["Popular in the Philippines","Mobile Legends: Bang Bang"],["SEA Games medal event","Since 2019"],["Asian Games medal event","Since 2022 (held in 2023)"]],
d:[["1972","Students hold a Spacewar! tournament at Stanford"],["1980","A Space Invaders championship draws about 10,000 players"],["2000s","StarCraft leagues make esports famous in South Korea"],["2011","Live streaming of games grows rapidly"],["2019","Esports become medal events at the SEA Games in the Philippines"],["2026","A Philippine team wins a sixth straight Mobile Legends world championship"]],
s2:[["The Philippines and Mobile Legends","Mobile Legends: Bang Bang, played on smartphones, is enormously popular in the Philippines and Indonesia. Philippine teams won six Mobile Legends world championships in a row, from the M2 championship in January 2021 to M7 in January 2026, and the national team, Sibol, has won international medals. Filipino players are among the most respected in mobile esports."],["The 2019 SEA Games","When the Philippines hosted the Southeast Asian Games in 2019, esports were among the medal events, one of the first times at a major multi-sport event, and Filipino players won several gold medals, including in Mobile Legends and Dota 2."],["Esports and the Olympics","Esports were a medal event at the 2022 Asian Games, held in Hangzhou, China, in 2023. The International Olympic Committee has explored creating Olympic Esports Games, though debates continue over which games should be included, since many involve combat and are owned by private companies."],["Big prizes","Some tournaments offer enormous prize money. The International, the world championship of Dota 2, has had prize pools of tens of millions of US dollars, funded partly by players buying items in the game. Top players can earn large incomes from prizes, salaries, sponsors and streaming."],["Health and balance","Long hours of gaming can cause eye strain, back and wrist problems and lack of sleep and exercise. Esports teams increasingly encourage fitness, healthy diets and mental health support. For most players, gaming is best enjoyed as a hobby balanced with school, work and other activities."],["Is it a sport?","People debate whether esports are true sports. Supporters point to the skill, strategy, teamwork, fast reactions and training they require, like chess, which is recognized as a sport by many bodies. Others argue that sports should involve physical exertion."]],
sp:["The first esports tournament","On 19 October 1972, students gathered at the Stanford Artificial Intelligence Laboratory in California for the Intergalactic Spacewar Olympics. They competed at Spacewar!, a game created at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in 1962 in which two spaceships battle while being pulled by a star’s gravity. The grand prize was a year’s subscription to Rolling Stone magazine, whose reporter covered the event. It is often considered the first esports tournament, half a century before world championships filled stadiums and drew millions of online viewers."],
g:[["Esports","Organized competitive video gaming"],["MOBA","Multiplayer online battle arena, a team strategy game"],["Prize pool","The total prize money in a tournament"],["Streaming","Broadcasting live over the internet"]],
f:["The first esports tournament was held in 1972.","Esports were medal events at the 2019 SEA Games.","Philippine teams won six Mobile Legends world titles in a row."],
fun:"The prize at the first esports tournament was a magazine subscription.",
tl:"Ang esports ay organisadong paligsahan sa video game. Napakasikat ng Mobile Legends sa Pilipinas, at nanalo ang mga koponang Pilipino ng anim na sunod na kampeonato sa mundo. Naging medal event ang esports sa SEA Games 2019."});
